AP Log Analyzer — Validation Pack
Three layers: (1) make the prompt hard to misread, (2) audit the code against the prompt, (3) run known-answer fixtures. Layer 3 is the only one that actually proves anything.
Layer 1 — Make the prompt verifiable before you send it
Append this to the consolidated prompt. It converts prose requirements into a machine-checkable contract.
IMPLEMENTATION CONTRACT

Before writing code, output a REQUIREMENTS TABLE: one row per numbered
requirement in Parts 1-6 and Appendices A-D, with the function name
that will implement it. I will review this before you write code.

Every requirement must map to exactly one named function. If two
requirements conflict, STOP and ask - do not silently pick one.

Code structure (non-negotiable):
  parse.js       delimiter sniff, encoding, channel mapping, hard-fail
  states.js      operating-state classifier, one pure function
  pulls.js       pull detection + scoreability gate
  rules/*.js     one file per rule, each exporting
                 evaluate(samples, states, ctx) -> Finding[]
  findings.js    Finding type; construction FAILS if any required
                 context field is missing
  report.js      renders sections in fixed order

A Finding object MUST NOT be constructible without: timestamp, rpm,
gear, boost, load, throttle, state, observed, expected, ruleId.
Enforce in the constructor. Throw, do not default.

No rule may read raw samples directly. Rules receive only samples
already tagged with state. Enforce by passing a frozen view.

Every threshold must be a named constant in a single config module,
with a comment citing which prompt section it came from. No magic
numbers inline.

Output at the end: a COVERAGE REPORT listing any requirement you did
not implement and why. An empty list is only acceptable if true.
Layer 2 — Audit prompt (run in a fresh session, no code context)
Paste the consolidated spec plus the generated source into a clean chat. A fresh model is a better auditor than the one that wrote it.
You are auditing an implementation against a specification. You did
not write this code. Be adversarial.

For each numbered requirement in the spec, output:
  ID | requirement (short) | IMPLEMENTED / PARTIAL / MISSING / WRONG
  | file:line | evidence

Rules for your verdicts:
- IMPLEMENTED requires you to quote the line that does it.
- If you cannot find the code, say MISSING. Do not assume it exists
  elsewhere.
- PARTIAL if the threshold, unit, or gating condition differs from
  the spec in any way, including sign.

Then answer specifically:
1. List every numeric literal in the rule logic. For each, name the
   spec section it comes from, or flag it as UNSOURCED.
2. Find every place a sample count is used where a time delta is
   required. Sample rate is variable (0.003-0.102s).
3. Find every comparison against an absolute value where a commanded
   channel exists (AFR, boost, fueling).
4. Find every rule that can fire on a single sample.
5. Find every code path that can emit a PASS or GOOD when the
   underlying channel is missing or the log has no WOT pull.
6. Confirm boost math is done in absolute pressure and that missing
   barometric pressure marks results UNVALIDATED.
7. Confirm knock event counting collapses monotonic decay into one
   event, and show the code that does it.
8. Confirm the NOT EVALUATED section can never be silently empty.

Do not suggest improvements. Only report conformance.
Layer 3 — Known-answer fixtures
Ten synthetic AP-format CSVs are provided, written in latin-1 with real degree symbols and jittered sample intervals (0.003-0.102s). expected_results.json holds the assertions. Every fixture is designed so that the WRONG implementation produces a specific, recognisable failure.
ID	Fixture	Must produce	Failure signature
F01	Cruise only, one FBK -1.05 with 3-sample decay	0 pulls; exactly 1 knock event classified CRUISE_NOISE, informational; NOT EVALUATED non-empty	Reports 4 events (decay counted separately) or reports LOAD_KNOCK
F02	Throttle stab: 8 samples >90%, 0.2s, peak 0.4 psi	0 pulls detected	Detects a pull and grades it
F03	Fuel-cut recovery: AFR 15.97 @ 0.18 psi, cmd 14.70; AFC -25.8% @ 1.8% IDC	0 AFR findings, 0 fuel-trim findings	Flags dangerous lean condition and/or fuel trim fault
F04	Clean 3rd-gear pull; target deliberately ramps 17->12 psi above 5000 rpm	1 pull, gear 3, no knock, no AFR finding, no overshoot finding, GOOD populated	False overshoot from collapsing target
F05	Real load knock, FBK -3.5 sustained, DAM holds 1.000	1 pull; LOAD_KNOCK; severity BAD (not UGLY); full context incl. IAT at pull start	Severity inflated to UGLY, or context fields missing
F06	True overshoot, 2.1 psi over target sustained in WOT_STEADY	1 overshoot finding; names P0234 / P226B	Missed because averaging diluted it
F07	DAM 1.000 -> 0.938, boost target drops 17 -> 13	UGLY; DAM<1.0 flagged; boost/AFR cross-check performed; target drop identified as failsafe	Reports boost tracking as GOOD, or treats target drop as tune intent
F08	Knock channels absent from header	CANNOT EVALUATE - monitor not logged	Says 'no knock detected'
F09	Semicolon-delimited	Parses; 0 pulls	Parse failure or single-column garbage
F10	No barometric channel	All boost error figures marked UNVALIDATED	Confident boost verdict with no baro
The assertion that matters
Each test asserts two things: that the correct finding appears, and that incorrect ones do not. Write the negative assertion first. A test suite that only checks for expected findings will pass on an over-sensitive analyzer, which is exactly the failure mode you are trying to eliminate.
Layer 4 — Acceptance gate on your real logs
•	Run log 3 (cruise-only) first. Expected: DRIVE TYPE = cruise only, PULLS empty, UGLY none, BAD none, GOOD near-empty, NOT EVALUATED long. Anything in GOOD or UGLY means the state classifier is not working.
•	Run all four logs. Total UGLY findings across all four must be zero. You already know this car is healthy — DAM 1.000 everywhere. Any UGLY is a false positive by definition.
•	Cross-session check: the cruise -1.05 pattern should appear as recurring in the same RPM x load cell across logs, and should be reported as recurring-but-informational, not escalated.
•	Diff test: run the same log twice. Byte-identical reports. Any variance means non-deterministic ordering or floating-point accumulation.
•	Regression lock: once all ten fixtures pass, commit the expected output verbatim as golden files. Future changes diff against them.
Layer 5 — Cheap red flags to watch for in review
•	Any occurrence of 12.5, 11.5, 14.7, or 0.025 as an inline literal.
•	Any use of array index arithmetic to represent elapsed time.
•	A rule file that imports the raw CSV rows rather than the state-tagged view.
•	The string "no knock detected" existing anywhere in the codebase.
•	A findings array that can be empty while the report still prints a GOOD section.
•	utf-8 appearing in any read call.
